#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167558} :activity/id #uuid "a3dd6621-c82e-571c-bf04-7adea5a2cf8e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049899 :topic/id #uuid "1cb109e6-3987-5c41-9a55-81f03d70697e" :topic/math-academy-id 3274} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168157 :tutorial/content "When performing certain types of statistical tests (e.g., hypothesis testing), we often need to use standard cumulative distribution function (CDF) tables to carry out the test.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to construct CDF tables for the Poisson distribution and use them to calculate probabilities.\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of the Poisson distribution $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{\\lambda^x e^{-\\lambda}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda$ is a positive parameter.\n\nThe CDF of a discrete random variable $X$ is a function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's compute the values of $F(x)$ for the distribution $\\textrm{Po}(2)$ at $x=0$ and $x=1$.\n\nTo do that, we use the definition of the cumulative distribution function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(0) &= P(X \\le 0) \\\\\n&= P(X = 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{0} \\cdot e^{-2}}{0!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot e^{-2}}{1} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.1353 \\\\\nF(1) &= P(X \\le 1) \\\\\n&= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{0} \\cdot e^{-2}}{0!} + \\frac{2^{1} \\cdot e^{-2}}{1!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 \\cdot e^{-2}}{1} + \\frac{2 \\cdot e^{-2}}{1} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.4060\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe CDF data is usually presented as a table. If we were to continue this process and calculate $F(2), F(3), F(4)$, and $F(5)$, our table would look as follows:\n\n| $\\quad x \\quad$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X\\leq x)$ | $\\,0.1353\\,$ | $\\,0.4060\\,$ | $\\,0.6767\\,$ | $\\,0.8571\\,$ | $\\,0.9473\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,\\,\\,\\cdots\\,\\,\\,$ |\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $F(x)$ is an increasing function.\n- Since $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda)$ takes arbitrarily large values, we have $F(x) < 1$ for all $n$, and $F(x) \\to 1$ as $x\\to\\infty$." :tutorial/id #uuid "7f81a6db-33d2-5a6a-98e7-852037072330" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9443 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7a435309-f7d2-561b-9e2d-96419cf972f5" :step/math-academy-id 221725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167559}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166735 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166498 :question/id #uuid "d8c1a664-c40a-5525-b1c0-2f977c01077a" :question/math-academy-id "e-15057"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "387be734-719f-52cd-8079-50c744ecdd5e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049358}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167149}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Poisson CDF Table"} :step/id #uuid "33739b57-c32a-5436-8a2f-c7b0a78550cc" :step/math-academy-id 221726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166499 :question/id #uuid "a4d766f8-8e20-52a0-afa1-0eea74e6688e" :question/math-academy-id "e-15058"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "240ed915-56cd-5e22-8c7f-a36ebea90279" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167151}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Poisson Probability Using a CDF Table"} :step/id #uuid "eb901a88-539f-5c1d-8161-d1cc6b32b4bb" :step/math-academy-id 221729 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166737 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166500 :question/id #uuid "b73faba8-1021-5d9c-94f5-5e1b908397b0" :question/math-academy-id "e-